{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"I kept it tight because the prompt explicitly said `<=250 words` and `Output only the critique`.\n\nExpanded version: the plan is strong on story clarity and execution handoff, but the actual assembler has a couple of likely failure points. Biggest one: `-shortest` in the final ffmpeg call will probably cut the film when the delayed VO ends, likely before the 55s picture ends. Add `apad`/silence to the VO mix or remove `-shortest`. Second: the concat path assumes all generated MP4s are perfectly compatible; I’d normalize every clip with `setsar=1`, fixed fps, fixed timebase, and inspect for DTS warnings. Third: the TTS timing is hand-placed but not duration-aware, so lines may collide or land awkwardly unless the script probes each WAV and adjusts offsets.\n\nThe design’s strongest idea is the in-room demo narrating itself. The weakest execution choice is hard-cutting the demo full-frame; it weakens the “review room” conceit. Even a rough screen composite in Phase 1 would sell the story better. I’d also add visible peer-review artifacts: redlines, approval stamps, conflicting tribe annotations, or a “sent back” moment, so the orchestration layer becomes cinematic rather than implied."}}
